            <ArticleTitle>Poor left ventricular function is not a contraindication for robotic totally endoscopic coronary artery bypass grafting.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E152-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1532/HSF98.20091048</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Robotic technology has enabled performance of totally endoscopic coronary artery bypass grafting (TECABG). Published series on TECABG were primarily performed in low-risk patients, and little is known about the outcome after totally endoscopic coronary surgery in patients with severely impaired left ventricular function. We report successful endoscopic placement of a left internal mammary artery bypass graft to the left anterior descending artery using the daVinci robotic system in a patient with a severely reduced left ventricular ejection fraction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
